# Typed Signals

## Overview

When the code that builds a Signal and the Action that handles it define the
data separately, they drift apart: the sender sends `qty`, the Action expects
`quantity`, and the Turn fails at runtime. `Jido.SignalAction` puts both in
one module. One schema validates the Signal data and the Action input.

With a SignalAction, the sender and the route use the same module:

```text
{:ok, signal} = Typed.AddItem.new(%{sku: "tea"})   # validated Signal
route Typed.AddItem                                # route path = "cart.item.add"
```

This Livebook builds a cart Agent with one typed route. Then it adds a typed
quote request whose reply is built after the Agent commits.

| A SignalAction gives you | Through |
| --- | --- |
| Input contract | `type/0`, `new/2`, `new!/2`, `validate_data/1` |
| Action | `run/2`, validated by `Jido.Exec` |
| Typed reply | `reply/3`, when the module declares `output:` |
| Route | `route MySignalAction`, with the path from the input type |

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Define A SignalAction

A SignalAction needs `name`, a non-blank `description`, a positive `vsn`, and
`input:` with the Signal `type` and `schema`. `input:` also accepts
`default_source`, `dataschema`, and `datacontenttype`.

```elixir
defmodule Typed.AddItem do
  use Jido.SignalAction,
    name: "add_item",
    description: "Adds one item to the cart",
    vsn: 1,
    input: [
      type: "cart.item.add",
      default_source: "/guides/typed",
      schema:
        Zoi.object(%{
          sku: Zoi.string(),
          quantity: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.min(1) |> Zoi.default(1)
        })
    ]

  @impl true
  def run(%{sku: sku, quantity: quantity}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | items: Map.update(state.items, sku, quantity, &(&1 + quantity))}}
  end
end
```

The input schema is also the Action's parameter schema, so `run/2` receives
exactly the data that passed Signal validation.

## Build Input Signals

`new/2` validates data and builds the Signal. It runs nothing.

```elixir
{:ok, signal} = Typed.AddItem.new(%{sku: "tea"})

{signal.type, signal.source, signal.data}
```

The result is `{"cart.item.add", "/guides/typed", %{quantity: 1, sku: "tea"}}`.
The schema default set `quantity`, and `default_source` set the source.

Invalid data returns the Zoi issues and builds no Signal:

```elixir
{:error, issues} = Typed.AddItem.new(%{sku: 1})

Enum.map(issues, & &1.message)
```

The result is `["invalid type: expected string"]`. `new!/2` raises instead.

## Route By Module

Name the module in `route`. The path comes from the input type, so the route
and the Signal can't disagree.

```elixir
defmodule Typed.Cart do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "cart"

  agent do
    schema(
      Zoi.object(%{
        items: Zoi.map(Zoi.string(), Zoi.integer()) |> Zoi.default(%{}),
        quotes: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)
      })
    )
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/typed")
    route Typed.AddItem, as: :add_item
  end
end

agent = Typed.Cart.instantiate!(id: "cart-1")
{:ok, candidate, []} = Typed.Cart.cmd(agent, signal)

candidate.state
```

The result is `%{items: %{"tea" => 1}, quotes: 0}`.

A module route accepts `as:` and `defaults:`. The generated helper builds the
same Signal type:

```elixir
more = Typed.Cart.add_item_signal!(%{sku: "tea", quantity: 2})
{:ok, candidate, []} = Typed.Cart.cmd(candidate, more)

{more.type, candidate.state.items}
```

The result is `{"cart.item.add", %{"tea" => 3}}`.

> #### Direct routes and `output:` {: .warning}
>
> On a direct module route, `run/2` returns the complete Agent state, and
> `Jido.Exec` validates that return value against the Action's output schema.
> If the SignalAction declares `output:`, Jido checks your Agent state against
> the reply schema and every Turn fails. Leave `output:` off SignalActions
> that you route to directly. Route reply-producing SignalActions with `via:`,
> shown below.

## Typed Replies

A SignalAction with `output:` turns a result into a validated reply Signal.

```elixir
defmodule Typed.PriceQuote do
  use Jido.SignalAction,
    name: "price_quote",
    description: "Prices the cart",
    vsn: 1,
    input: [
      type: "cart.quote.request",
      default_source: "/guides/typed",
      schema: Zoi.object(%{items: Zoi.map(Zoi.string(), Zoi.integer())})
    ],
    output: [
      type: "cart.quote.ready",
      default_source: "/guides/typed",
      schema: Zoi.object(%{total_cents: Zoi.integer()})
    ]

  @impl true
  def run(%{items: items}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{total_cents: items |> Map.values() |> Enum.sum() |> Kernel.*(450)}}
  end
end
```

Run it with `Jido.Exec.run/4` and build the reply with `reply/3`:

```elixir
request = Typed.PriceQuote.new!(%{items: %{"tea" => 3}})
{:ok, result} = Jido.Exec.run(Typed.PriceQuote, request.data)
{:ok, reply} = Typed.PriceQuote.reply(request, result)

{reply.type, reply.data, reply.id == request.id,
 reply.extensions["jidocausationid"] == request.id}
```

The result is `{"cart.quote.ready", %{total_cents: 1350}, false, true}`. The
reply has its own `id`, keeps the request's trace, and records the request
`id` as its causation.

`reply/3` validates the reply data against `output:` and rejects a request of
the wrong type:

```elixir
{:error, wrong_request} = Typed.PriceQuote.reply(signal, result)

Exception.message(wrong_request)
```

The result is `"Request does not match the input Signal definition"`.
`new/2` and `reply/3` build Signals. Neither one sends anything.

## Route With via:

To have an Agent accept a quote request, commit a state change, and only
then compute and send the reply, route the request with `via:`:

```text
route Typed.PriceQuote, via: Typed.AcceptQuote
```

The path stays `"cart.quote.request"`, but the route runs `Typed.AcceptQuote`,
a normal Agent Action. It counts the request and returns a Directive that
carries it. A Plugin owns that Directive and runs the SignalAction after
commit.

```elixir
defmodule Typed.RequestQuote do
  defstruct [:request]
  use Jido.Agent.Directive
end

defmodule Typed.AcceptQuote do
  use Jido.Action, name: "accept_quote", schema: Zoi.object(%{items: Zoi.any()})

  @impl true
  def run(_params, %{agent_state: state, signal: request}) do
    {:ok, %{state | quotes: state.quotes + 1}, [%Typed.RequestQuote{request: request}]}
  end
end

defmodule Typed.Quoting do
  use Jido.Plugin

  @impl true
  def directives(_opts), do: [Typed.RequestQuote]

  @impl true
  def dispatch(_runtime, %Typed.RequestQuote{request: request}, context, _opts) do
    with {:ok, result} <- Jido.Exec.run(Typed.PriceQuote, request.data),
         {:ok, reply} <- Typed.PriceQuote.reply(request, result) do
      send(context.turn_context.reply_to, {:quote, reply})
      :ok
    end
  end
end

defmodule Typed.QuotingCart do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "quoting_cart", extensions: [Typed.Quoting]

  agent do
    schema(
      Zoi.object(%{
        items: Zoi.map(Zoi.string(), Zoi.integer()) |> Zoi.default(%{}),
        quotes: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)
      })
    )
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/typed")
    route Typed.AddItem, as: :add_item
    route Typed.PriceQuote, via: Typed.AcceptQuote, as: :request_quote
  end
end

quoting = Typed.QuotingCart.instantiate!(id: "cart-2", state: %{items: %{"tea" => 3}})
{:ok, accepted, [directive]} = Typed.QuotingCart.cmd(quoting, request)

{accepted.state.quotes, directive.__struct__}
```

The result is `{1, Typed.RequestQuote}`. `cmd/2` counted the request and
returned the Directive. It priced nothing, because pure evaluation never runs
Directives.

The Directive must have an owner. `Typed.Quoting` claims it in
`directives/1`. Without an owning Plugin, the Turn fails with
`"Agent Directive has no owner"`. [Custom Directives](plugin-custom-directives.livemd)
covers ownership and dispatch.

`Typed.QuotingCart` lists the Plugin in `extensions:` because it needs no
Agent-specific config. You can write `plugin(Typed.Quoting)` inside the
`agent` block instead, including in a Livebook cell.

## Run It Live

Start the Agent and send the request. `reply_to:` in the caller context tells
the Plugin where to send the reply.

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()
{:ok, cart} = Jido.start_agent(quoting)
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(cart)

{:ok, committed, [_directive]} =
  Jido.AgentServer.call(cart, request, context: %{reply_to: self()})

reply =
  receive do
    {:quote, reply} -> {reply.type, reply.data}
  after
    1_000 -> :no_reply
  end

{committed.state.quotes, reply}
```

The result is `{1, {"cart.quote.ready", %{total_cents: 1350}}}`.

The Server committed `quotes: 1` and replied to your call before the Plugin
priced the cart. If pricing fails, the committed state stays, and the failure
appears in `Jido.AgentServer.status/2` under `last_effect_outcome`.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(cart)
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

## Summary

- One SignalAction defines the input Signal, the Action, and an optional
  typed reply.
- `route MySignalAction` takes its path from the input type.
- Direct routes return complete Agent state: leave `output:` off them.
- `reply/3` builds a validated reply carrying the request's trace and
  causation.
- `via:` commits the state change first and defers the typed work to an owned
  Directive.

